From: Brick Linux <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv32: do not pad 64-bit syscall arguments

RISC-V passes 64-bit syscall arguments in consecutive registers without an
alignment pad, and Linux's riscv syscall wrapper reads one register per
argument. With __UCLIBC_SYSCALL_ALIGN_64BIT__ defined, ftruncate64(fd, 5)
reaches the kernel as length 0 (the pad), so `truncate -s 5` and busybox vi
(which writes, then ftruncates) leave empty files. pread64/pwrite64 offsets
are similarly broken.

--- a/libc/sysdeps/linux/riscv32/bits/uClibc_arch_features.h
+++ b/libc/sysdeps/linux/riscv32/bits/uClibc_arch_features.h
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@
 /* does your target use statx */
 #undef __UCLIBC_HAVE_STATX__
 
-#define __UCLIBC_SYSCALL_ALIGN_64BIT__
+#undef __UCLIBC_SYSCALL_ALIGN_64BIT__
 
 /* does your target have a broken create_module() ? */
 #define __UCLIBC_BROKEN_CREATE_MODULE__
